/// \defgroup grp_w3_online_preferences Quick-match, custom-match and LAN preferences
/// \ingroup grp_network
/// \brief The per-user settings that the online quick-match screen, the
///        GameSpy custom-match lobby and the LAN lobby remember between
///        sessions, and the small accessors that read and write them.
///
/// \par The preference-file model
/// QuickMatchPreferences, CustomMatchPreferences and LANPreferences are all
/// UserPreferences: an in-memory map from key to value, both narrow
/// AsciiStrings, loaded from one plain `key = value` text file when the
/// object is constructed and saved back only when UserPreferences::write is
/// called. \ref sub_engine_core explains where those files live and why a
/// BIG archive cannot supply them. Most accessors in this group follow one
/// pattern, read from the matched bodies:
/// - A getter looks its key up in the map directly. When the key is absent
///   it returns a default compiled into the getter; otherwise it converts
///   the stored text with the C runtime (atoi for numbers, a
///   case-insensitive comparison for flags) or returns a copy of it.
/// - A setter formats its value as decimal text ("%d", so a Bool becomes
///   "1" or "0") and assigns it to the key, creating the key if needed.
///   Nothing reaches the disk until the owner calls write.
///
/// The exceptions: QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected stores the literal
/// "1" or "0" under a key encoded from the map name;
/// QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder and
/// CustomMatchPreferences::setLastLadder store the address as given; and
/// LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir looks up a key built per user and
/// compares it with "yes".
///
/// These accessors index the map themselves rather than going through
/// UserPreferences's virtual typed accessors (getInt, setInt and the
/// others). That matters in BFME 2, where the LAN preferences, and the
/// object SetUpGameSpy reads the custom-match filters from (it loads
/// `CustomPref<profile id>.ini`), are built on a mode-keyed UserPreferences
/// base that the reconstruction calls GameModePreferences (a descriptive
/// name; no retail spelling is known). Its typed accessors store each key
/// as `<prefix>:<key>`, where the prefix is "Rts" for mode 0, "Strat" for
/// mode 1 and empty for any other mode. SetUpGameSpy builds its object with
/// mode -1, so that object's typed keys take the form `:<key>`, which still
/// differs from the unprefixed keys that the accessors in this group read
/// and write.
///
/// \par Files and keys
/// | Class | File (BFME 2) | Keys handled here |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | QuickMatchPreferences | `Online Files\QMPref<profile id>.ini` | MaxPing, MaxPoints, MinPoints, WaitTime, MaxDisconnects, NumPlayers, LastLadderAddr, LastLadderPort, one key per map |
/// | CustomMatchPreferences | `Online Files\CustomPref<profile id>.ini` | LastLadderAddr, LastLadderPort, DisallowAsianText, DisallowNonAsianText, UseLongGameList |
///
/// The profile id is the local player's GameSpy profile id, taken from
/// TheGameSpyInfo when the object is constructed, so each online account
/// has its own files. The table lists only the keys these
/// accessors handle; the classes keep other keys through other members.
///
/// LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir reads a per-user UseSystemMapDir key.
/// Which file holds it depends on the class that body really belongs to:
/// `NetworkPref.ini` if it is LANPreferences's, as the ledger files it, or
/// `Skirmish.ini` if it is SkirmishPreferences's (see the warning on
/// LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir).
///
/// \par Compared with Zero Hour
/// Verified against both bodies:
/// - Zero Hour keeps the online files in a `GeneralsOnline` folder and the
///   LAN file as `Network.ini`; BFME 2 uses `Online Files` and
///   `NetworkPref.ini`.
/// - QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPing defaults to -1 instead of Zero
///   Hour's 5, and CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowAsianText defaults
///   to false instead of Zero Hour's language-dependent default. Open-BFME-1's
///   reconstruction of BFME 1 has the same two defaults as BFME 2.
/// - BFME 2's LANPreferences takes a game-mode argument and is built on
///   the mode-keyed base described above (see LANPreferences).
///
/// \par Modding notes
/// - These files hold front-end state only: player choices and remembered
///   values such as the quick-match rank keys. No accessor here touches
///   game logic, so editing or deleting a file changes what the screens
///   preselect or filter, not the rules of a match. With a file deleted,
///   every accessor below returns its documented default.
/// - Key names and defaults are compiled into the accessors. Several stored
///   numbers are list positions rather than quantities (see
///   QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPing and
///   QuickMatchPreferences::getNumPlayers), so changing the lists those
///   screens build reinterprets values that players have already saved.
///
/// \par Remastering notes
/// - The online file names embed the GameSpy profile id. A replacement
///   online service needs its own stable per-account id, or players lose
///   these settings when they move to it.
/// - A ladder is remembered as an address string plus a 16-bit port
///   (QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder). A replacement ladder service
///   that identifies ladders another way needs new keys, and old files
///   then point at servers that no longer exist.
///
/// \see \ref sub_network, \ref sub_engine_core


/// \class QuickMatchPreferences
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief The online quick-match screen's remembered search settings:
///        players per side, ping limit, last ladder, map choices and the
///        rest, one file per GameSpy profile.
///
/// A UserPreferences whose constructor loads
/// `Online Files\QMPref<profile id>.ini`, using the profile id that
/// TheGameSpyInfo reports (Zero Hour: `GeneralsOnline\QMPref<id>.ini`).
/// Its accessors are thin wrappers over the preference map, each bound to
/// one key, described in the group overview.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// The online quick-match screen, AptOnline::OnlineQuickMatch, owns one as a
/// member, so the file is read when the screen is built; the screen fills
/// its player-count and ping lists from it. Saving uses a separate
/// short-lived object: the screen's option-saving code constructs a fresh
/// QuickMatchPreferences, stores the last ladder, the player count, the
/// ping limit, the side and the colour, and calls write. The two objects do not share state, so a
/// value set on one is not visible through the other until the file is
/// read again.
///
/// \par The stored values
/// - NumPlayers and MaxPing are positions in the screen's drop-down lists,
///   not a player count or milliseconds (see the getters).
/// - LastLadderAddr and LastLadderPort identify the ladder last chosen;
///   empty and 0 mean none.
/// - One key per map, derived from the map's name, holds a selected flag
///   (QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected). Zero Hour: the quick-match map
///   list records the player's ticked maps there; BFME 2's caller is not
///   traced.
/// - MaxPoints, MinPoints, WaitTime and MaxDisconnects have accessors but
///   BFME 2's save routine does not store them. Zero Hour's quick-match
///   menu saved MaxDisconnects as a position in its disconnect-limit list
///   and sent MaxPoints and MinPoints, clamped, as the point-percentage
///   limits of its match request. WaitTime's unit is not established:
///   Zero Hour's shipped code never uses the value (the code that showed
///   and saved it is commented out), BFME 2's quick-match request is built
///   with a fixed widen time of 0, and no BFME 2 caller of the WaitTime
///   accessors is traced. To see which BFME 2 code calls an accessor, run
///   `tools/callers_of.py` on its ledger address.
///
/// The colour and side accessors, and the getters and setters for two rank
/// keys that Zero Hour's class lacks, go through the virtual typed accessors
/// instead and are not covered here.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder,
///      QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected, CustomMatchPreferences


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPing(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the remembered ping-limit choice, or -1 when none is
///        stored.
///
/// The value is a position in the quick-match screen's ping list, not a
/// time. The screen builds that list with one entry per 100 ms (labelled
/// with the "GUI:TimeInMilliseconds" string) below the GameSpy
/// configuration's ping timeout, then a final "GUI:ANY" entry, and selects
/// the stored position. A negative or out-of-range value, including the -1
/// default, selects the final "any" entry.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Looks up MaxPing and converts the text with atoi; text that is not a
/// number therefore reads as 0, the first entry.
///
/// \return The stored list position, or -1 when the key is absent.
///
/// \note Zero Hour returns 5 when the key is absent, and its screen clamps
///       a negative value to the first entry rather than the last. With
///       BFME 2's default a player who never saved a choice starts on
///       "any".
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxPing


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxPing(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the ping-limit choice as decimal text under MaxPing.
///
/// The quick-match screen's option-saving code passes the selected position
/// of its ping list. The change is in memory until write is called.
///
/// \param val The list position to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPing


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPoints(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored MaxPoints value, or 1000 when none is stored.
///
/// Looks up MaxPoints and converts it with atoi. Zero Hour used this as the
/// upper point-percentage limit of a quick-match request (raised to at
/// least 100 before sending), with the same default of 1000.
///
/// \return The stored number, 1000 when the key is absent, or 0 when the
///         text is not a number.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxPoints,
///      QuickMatchPreferences::getMinPoints


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxPoints(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the MaxPoints value as decimal text.
///
/// \param val The value to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPoints


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getMinPoints(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored MinPoints value, or 0 when none is stored.
///
/// Looks up MinPoints and converts it with atoi. Zero Hour used this as the
/// lower point-percentage limit of a quick-match request (capped at 100
/// before sending).
///
/// \return The stored number, or 0 when the key is absent or not a number.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setMinPoints,
///      QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxPoints


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setMinPoints(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the MinPoints value as decimal text.
///
/// \param val The value to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getMinPoints


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getWaitTime(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored WaitTime value, or 0 when none is stored.
///
/// Looks up WaitTime and converts it with atoi. Zero Hour: the quick-match
/// menu's code that showed and saved this value, and sent the same text
/// box's value as the match request's widen time, is commented out; the
/// live request sends a widen time of 0. BFME 2's quick-match request is
/// also built with a fixed widen time of 0, and no BFME 2 caller of this
/// getter is traced, so the value's unit is not established.
///
/// \return The stored number, or 0 when the key is absent or not a number.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setWaitTime


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setWaitTime(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the WaitTime value as decimal text.
///
/// \param val The value to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getWaitTime


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxDisconnects(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored MaxDisconnects value, or 0 when none is
///        stored.
///
/// Looks up MaxDisconnects and converts it with atoi. Zero Hour stored a
/// position in its disconnect-limit list here, position 0 being "any".
///
/// \return The stored number, or 0 when the key is absent or not a number.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxDisconnects


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setMaxDisconnects(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the MaxDisconnects value as decimal text.
///
/// \param val The value to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getMaxDisconnects


/// \fn Int QuickMatchPreferences::getNumPlayers(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the remembered players-per-side choice, or 0 when none is
///        stored.
///
/// The value is a position in the quick-match screen's player-count list.
/// BFME 2's screen builds that list with two entries, one versus one and
/// two versus two (labelled with the "GUI:PlayersVersusPlayers" string),
/// and selects the stored position, treating a negative value as 0. So 0,
/// the default, means one versus one.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Looks up NumPlayers and converts it with atoi.
///
/// \return The stored list position, or 0 when the key is absent or not a
///         number.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's list has four entries, one versus one to four versus
///       four.
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setNumPlayers


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setNumPlayers(Int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores the players-per-side choice as decimal text under
///        NumPlayers.
///
/// The quick-match screen's option-saving code passes the selected position
/// of its player-count list.
///
/// \param val The list position to store. No range check is made.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::getNumPlayers


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder(const AsciiString &addr, UnsignedShort port)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Remembers the ladder the player last chose, by its server address
///        and port.
///
/// The quick-match screen's option-saving code looks the selected ladder up
/// in the ladder list and passes its address and port, then writes the file
/// at once; when no ladder matches it stores an empty address and port 0.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Stores the address unchanged under LastLadderAddr and the port as
/// decimal text under LastLadderPort. The address is not escaped, unlike
/// the map keys of QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected.
///
/// \param addr The ladder's address as text; an empty string means no
///             ladder.
/// \param port The ladder's port; 0 with an empty address means no ladder.
///
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr,
///      CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderPort,
///      CustomMatchPreferences::setLastLadder


/// \fn void QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected(const AsciiString &mapName, Bool selected)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores a per-map selected flag.
///
/// Zero Hour: the quick-match map list records the player's ticked maps
/// here. BFME 2's caller is not traced.
///
/// Each map gets its own key: the map name passed through
/// AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable, which turns every character other than a
/// letter or digit into an underscore and two hex digits, so a path with
/// spaces, dots or backslashes still forms a single key in the file. The
/// value is "1" or "0". QuickMatchPreferences::isMapSelected builds the same
/// key and treats a map with no entry as selected, so only a map whose
/// flag is false needs a stored "0".
///
/// \param mapName The map's name as the map list identifies it (Zero Hour
///                passes the map file name).
/// \param selected True to store "1", false to store "0".
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::isMapSelected, AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable


/// \class CustomMatchPreferences
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief The GameSpy custom-match lobby's remembered settings, including
///        the Asian-text and non-Asian-text filter flags that SetUpGameSpy
///        copies into TheGameSpyInfo when the online services start.
///
/// In Zero Hour a UserPreferences that loads
/// `GeneralsOnline\CustomPref<profile id>.ini` and remembers lobby settings
/// including the last ladder, the preferred map, colour and faction, and
/// the filters below. In BFME 2, the object SetUpGameSpy reads the two
/// text-filter getters from is built on a mode-keyed UserPreferences base
/// (the reconstruction calls it GameModePreferences; no retail name is
/// known): SetUpGameSpy constructs it through a constructor that takes a
/// game-mode argument (it passes -1) and loads
/// `Online Files\CustomPref<profile id>.ini`. Identifying that constructor
/// as CustomMatchPreferences's is an inference from the file name it loads
/// and from SetUpGameSpy calling the custom-match getters on the object it
/// builds. Which objects the class's other accessors run on is not traced.
///
/// \par How it is used
/// SetUpGameSpy, which starts the online services, constructs one, reads
/// CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowAsianText and
/// CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowNonAsianText, and copies both flags
/// into TheGameSpyInfo before creating the GameSpy configuration object.
/// Zero Hour applies the flags in two places, with the same rule in both:
/// when the first flag is set, text holding any character outside the
/// first 256 code points is rejected; when only the second is set, text
/// holding no such character is rejected. BFME 2's traced uses include the
/// same two; whether other parts of its online code also read the flags is
/// not traced:
/// - GameSpyInfo::addChat drops chat messages from other players whose
///   text the rule rejects; the player's own messages are not checked.
///   BFME 2's body reads the flags as two GameSpyInfo data members; that
///   they are the members the two setters write is inferred from Zero
///   Hour's GameSpyInfo, not traced through BFME 2's setters.
/// - The custom-match screen's game list (Zero Hour: the lobby game list in
///   LobbyUtils.cpp) leaves out games whose names the rule rejects. BFME 2
///   reads the flags through the two GameSpyInfo virtual functions that
///   follow the two setters SetUpGameSpy calls. Zero Hour declares
///   GameSpyInfo's getDisallowAsianText and getDisallowNonAsianText getters
///   in those positions, so identifying them as the getters is an
///   inference.
///
/// \par Class attribution
/// Zero Hour gives QuickMatchPreferences and CustomMatchPreferences
/// identical setLastLadder, getLastLadderAddr and getLastLadderPort
/// members, so their bytes alone cannot tell the twins apart. See
/// CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr for what this means for
/// the two getters.
///
/// \see SetUpGameSpy, QuickMatchPreferences, LANPreferences


/// \fn AsciiString CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the remembered ladder address, or an empty string when
///        none is stored.
///
/// Looks up LastLadderAddr and returns a copy of the stored text, or
/// AsciiString::TheEmptyString when the key is absent.
///
/// \return The ladder address as stored; empty means no ladder.
///
/// \warning The owning class is not settled. Zero Hour's quick-match and
///          custom-match versions are identical, and in retail this body
///          sits among the QuickMatchPreferences accessors rather than
///          beside CustomMatchPreferences's other members. The ledger files
///          it under CustomMatchPreferences; the name may change.
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderPort,
///      QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder


/// \fn UnsignedShort CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderPort(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the remembered ladder port, or 0 when none is stored.
///
/// Looks up LastLadderPort and converts it with atoi; the result is
/// truncated to 16 bits.
///
/// \return The stored port, or 0 when the key is absent or not a number.
///
/// \warning The owning class is not settled, for the reasons given on
///          CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr; the name may change.
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr,
///      QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder


/// \fn void CustomMatchPreferences::setLastLadder(const AsciiString &addr, UnsignedShort port)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Remembers the custom-match lobby's last ladder by address and
///        port.
///
/// Same body as QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder: the address is stored
/// unchanged under LastLadderAddr and the port as decimal text under
/// LastLadderPort. The other copy is QuickMatchPreferences's by its
/// caller: the quick-match option-saving code calls it on the same object
/// as setters that only QuickMatchPreferences has. This copy takes the
/// custom-match name by elimination, and it sits among the custom-match
/// accessors in retail.
///
/// \param addr The ladder's address as text; empty means no ladder.
/// \param port The ladder's port.
///
/// \see QuickMatchPreferences::setLastLadder,
///      CustomMatchPreferences::getLastLadderAddr


/// \fn Bool CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowAsianText(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored DisallowAsianText flag; false when nothing
///        is stored.
///
/// SetUpGameSpy reads this when the online services start and passes it to
/// TheGameSpyInfo. In the traced uses, BFME 2, like Zero Hour, then hides
/// games in the game list, and drops other players' chat messages, whose
/// text holds any character outside the first 256 code points (see
/// CustomMatchPreferences for the traced uses and the inferences behind
/// them).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Looks up DisallowAsianText and returns true only when the stored text
/// equals "1", compared without regard to case.
///
/// \return True when the stored value is "1"; false when it is anything
///         else or absent.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's default depends on the language in the registry:
///       false for Chinese and Korean, true otherwise, because (its
///       source comment says) English Windows 98 had no Unicode font by
///       default. BFME 2 always defaults to false.
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::setDisallowAsianText,
///      CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowNonAsianText


/// \fn void CustomMatchPreferences::setDisallowAsianText(Bool val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores a flag under DisallowAsianText as "1" or "0".
///
/// \param val True to store "1", false to store "0". See
///            CustomMatchPreferences for how the flag is applied.
///
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowAsianText


/// \fn Bool CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowNonAsianText(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns the stored DisallowNonAsianText flag; false when nothing
///        is stored.
///
/// SetUpGameSpy reads this alongside
/// CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowAsianText and passes it to
/// TheGameSpyInfo. In the traced uses, BFME 2, like Zero Hour, applies it
/// only when the DisallowAsianText flag is clear, to hide games in the game
/// list, and drop other players' chat messages, whose text holds no
/// character outside the first 256 code points (see CustomMatchPreferences
/// for the traced uses and the inferences behind them).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Looks up DisallowNonAsianText and returns true only when the stored text
/// equals "1", compared without regard to case. The default, false, is the
/// same as Zero Hour's.
///
/// \return True when the stored value is "1"; false when it is anything
///         else or absent.
///
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::setDisallowNonAsianText


/// \fn void CustomMatchPreferences::setDisallowNonAsianText(Bool val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores a flag under DisallowNonAsianText as "1" or "0".
///
/// \param val True to store "1", false to store "0". See
///            CustomMatchPreferences for how the flag is applied.
///
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences::getDisallowNonAsianText


/// \fn void CustomMatchPreferences::setUsesLongGameList(Bool val)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Stores a flag under UseLongGameList as "1" or "0".
///
/// The key is UseLongGameList (not "UsesLongGameList"). No BFME 2 caller is
/// traced. Zero Hour: the lobby menu's shutdown stores false when a
/// game-info list box exists and true otherwise. Its GetGameInfoListBox
/// always returns NULL, so the shipped game always stores true, and the
/// matching getter ignores the stored value and always reports the long
/// list.
///
/// \param val True to store "1", false to store "0" (Zero Hour always
///            passes true).
///
/// \see CustomMatchPreferences


/// \class LANPreferences
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief The LAN lobby's remembered settings, kept in their own file.
///
/// Zero Hour: a UserPreferences that loads `Network.ini` and remembers LAN
/// lobby settings including the player name, colour, faction, map and
/// remote addresses. In BFME 2 its constructor takes a game-mode argument,
/// and it is built on a mode-keyed UserPreferences base (the reconstruction
/// calls it GameModePreferences; no retail name is known). That base's
/// typed accessors store each key as `<prefix>:<key>`, the prefix being
/// "Rts" for mode 0, "Strat" for mode 1 and empty for any other mode, so
/// one file can hold separate settings per game mode. The constructor then
/// loads `NetworkPref.ini`
/// through LANPreferences::loadFromIniFile rather than inline as Zero
/// Hour's does.
///
/// Only LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir is documented in this group.
///
/// \see LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir, CustomMatchPreferences


/// \fn Bool LANPreferences::usesSystemMapDir(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_online_preferences
/// \brief Returns whether the per-user UseSystemMapDir key holds "yes";
///        true when the key is absent.
///
/// No BFME 2 caller is traced. Zero Hour: the map-selection screens call
/// their preferences' usesSystemMapDir to choose between the shipped maps
/// and the maps in the player's own folder.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The key is built by SkirmishPreferences::buildProfileKey from
/// "UseSystemMapDir", which prefixes it with the integer the
/// SkirmishPreferences object was constructed with (its meaning is not
/// established) and the current user name in its stored, quoted-printable
/// encoded form: the CurrentUserName value, which
/// SkirmishPreferences::setCurrentUserName encodes with
/// UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable before storing it. The key therefore has
/// the form `:<n>:<encoded user>:UseSystemMapDir`, so the choice is kept
/// per user; a hand-edited file must use the encoded name. The
/// stored text must equal "yes", compared without regard to case, to
/// return true; any other text returns false. This is a different
/// convention from the "1" and "0" of the other flags in this group.
///
/// \return True when the key is absent or holds "yes"; false otherwise.
///
/// \warning The owning class is not settled. The key builder it calls reads
///          SkirmishPreferences's own integer tag and current-user-name
///          members, which BFME 2's LANPreferences is not known to have
///          (it is built on a mode-keyed UserPreferences base that the
///          reconstruction calls GameModePreferences; no retail name is
///          known); in retail the body sits among
///          SkirmishPreferences's members; and Zero Hour's
///          SkirmishPreferences::usesSystemMapDir has the same logic as its
///          LANPreferences version. The ledger files it under
///          LANPreferences, as in Zero Hour's LAN lobby source, but it may
///          belong to SkirmishPreferences, and the name may change.
/// \see SkirmishPreferences::buildProfileKey, UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable
